How I built ubuntu-attacker. Same base as the SOC-server 
User account :  analyst

Tools:      sudo apt update
            sudo apt install -y \
            nmap \
            net-tools \
            curl \
            wget \
            git \
            python3 \
            python3-pip \
            netcat-openbsd \
            tcpdump

nmap is the main one. Port and service scanning, which is usually the first thing
an attacker does and should be one of the first things a SIEM notices. 

tcpdump is so I can capture what I'm actually sending. This matters because I want to compare
the traffic I generated against what ended up in the logs. 
If something I did doesnt show up anywhere, that gap is the interesting part. 

netcat is for making simple raw TCP connections I can control precisely and curl
and wget are for generating web traffic once the victim has something listening. 

net-tools is old but netstat is stil convenient. python2 and pip are for automating scenarios
later and for the alert parsin script. git so I can write notes from the VM itself. 

